+## Summary
+
+The plan held. Four `feat` commits, no `mech` ones.
+
+Staff create percentage-off codes with `POST /admin/discount-codes` (`{"code", "percent", "expires_on"}`); creating one is recorded in the audit log. Customers enter a code at checkout with `discount_code`, and it shows on the order as an adjustment of kind `discount_code` (e.g. `10% off with SUMMER10`). The codes are stored in a new `discount_codes` table (migration 008). The API docs and changelog are updated.
+
+Decisions to check:
+
+- **Percent is 1 to 50.** I used the same cap as employee discounts. It's `MAX_PERCENT` in `shop/domain/discount_codes.py`.
+- **Expiry is a date, inclusive, in UK time.** A code works until the end of its `expires_on` day in `Europe/London` (new `clock.today()`). Staff can't create a code whose expiry date has already passed.
+- **Codes are case-insensitive.** They're stored in upper case, and are 3 to 20 letters and digits.
+- **Codes stack.** A code works alongside an employee discount and loyalty points, and each percentage is of the merchandise. An employee with 50% off, using a 50% code and redeeming points, would go over the merchandise. They get the existing 422 "Adjustments can't come to more than the merchandise".
+- **Unknown or expired codes are a 422** (`validation_failed`), not a 404, and nothing is charged.
+- **Built for later kinds, but only lightly.** Codes have a `kind` column (only `percent` for now), and `percent` is nullable in the table. That lets a fixed amount off become another kind without a new table. Free shipping and buy-one-get-one-free don't fit `Adjustment`, which only comes off the merchandise, so I didn't design for them.
+- **Not built:** listing, editing or ending codes early; usage limits; showing the code in the cart preview (which doesn't show the other discounts either).
